# EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR: A '{$' inside '{$...}': the interpolation before it is not closed
# EXPECT_ERROR_COUNT: 1
#~
An interpolation ends at its first '}', so it cannot hold another: a '{$' met while one is open
means the first was never closed. The string below used to compile. The analyzer started the
expression again at the second '{$' and kept the text before it a second time, so it printed
"cost: cost: {$ then 5". A string of N such openers held N copies of its beginning (5,000 of
them a 25 million character literal). Two interpolations side by side, and text with a '{' or a
'$' in it, are not this and still compile (core_string_interp_*).
~#
class BadInterpOpenTwice {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		price := 5;
		"cost: {$ then {$price}"->PrintLine();
	}
}
